│ Okay, let’s address this persistent issue.   │
│ The core problem is that Django’s template   │
│ tag engine doesn’t handle keyword-only       │
│ arguments with defaults correctly when used  │
│ in simple tags. Specifically, it generates   │
│ misleading error messages. The issue arises  │
│ because the error message doesn’t clearly    │
│ indicate that multiple keyword arguments are │
│ being provided when only one is expected.    │
│                                              │
│ Reasoning:                                   │
│                                              │
│ The hello and hi tags demonstrate this       │
│ issue. When a user supplies a keyword        │
│ argument (e.g., greeting='hi') and then      │
│ attempts to supply another keyword argument  │
│ (e.g., greeting='hello'), the engine         │
│ incorrectly reports an "unexpected keyword   │
│ argument" error instead of a "multiple       │
│ values for keyword argument" error. This is  │
│ due to how Django handles default values and │
│ keyword arguments in simple tags. The fix    │
│ involves modifying the error message to be   │
│ more informative.                            │
│                                              │
│ Patch:                                       │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
