│ Okay, let’s address this persistent issue.   │
│ The core problem is that Django’s template   │
│ tag engine isn’t correctly handling          │
│ keyword-only arguments with default values   │
│ when used in simple tags. Specifically, it’s │
│ generating misleading error messages. The    │
│ issue arises because the tag engine doesn’t  │
│ clearly distinguish between a missing        │
│ keyword argument and an unexpected keyword   │
│ argument when a default is provided.         │
│                                              │
│ Reasoning:                                   │
│                                              │
│ The error messages generated by the hello    │
│ and hi tags are incorrect. They should       │
│ indicate that a keyword argument is being    │
│ supplied when it’s not expected, rather than │
│ stating that an unexpected keyword argument  │
│ was received. The fix involves modifying the │
│ error message generation logic within        │
│ django/template/loader.py to provide a more  │
│ informative error message that accurately    │
│ reflects the situation.  We need to change   │
│ the error message to explicitly state that   │
│ the keyword argument is not allowed when a   │
│ default value is already provided.           │
│                                              │
│ Patch:                                       │
│                                              │
│ ┏━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓ │
